analysis:
This batch only changes documentation: docs/developer/build.md (head-side excerpts, lines 449-1223) and docs/developer/test.md (patch adding a section after line 1). No executable code, auth logic, crypto, billing or config is changed, so I found no concrete regression in this batch.

The test.md patch documents a Stage C provider endpoint-resumption feature (AppAttestResume*, AppAttestResumeTransportTests, shared Go vector app-attest-resumption-v1.json). It says a provider can reconnect over a fresh WebSocket socket nonce with the same process, endpoint and model/engine identity, and without a new Apple assertion. Its tests assert unchanged Apple assertion counters and rejection of stale callbacks. TB-011 and T-053 currently say serving permission is bound to one current connection, process endpoint and policy generation. The production resumption code is not in this batch. Later passes should check whether the resumption verifier:
- binds the resume proof to the account, machine, credential, endpoint, policy and qualification generations;
- rejects replay after a revocation, a release de-qualification or a lease expiry;
- keeps final-dispatch freshness checks (T-053, A-016);
- fences against a second concurrent socket.
The threat model's Apple-reassertion and connection-binding wording may need a resumption note if this changes it.

The docs also describe a new provider logout flow that stops the provider, revokes the token and deletes local state (ProviderLogoutTests, LogoutCommandTests). It requires exact HTTP 204 and refuses redirects. A later pass should confirm that the token (A-008) and credential revocation cannot be bypassed or left half-done.

The native-qualification harness is documented as using the real Apple App Attest service, an ephemeral Secure Enclave signer, a 0600 auth-token and a 0700 directory. It is isolated from installed state. Staging and seeding of platform test data should stay out of production.

build.md changes are pin and procedure text: the MLX submodule pins, the runtime-smoke markers and the threat-model review tooling. Only the doc-claimed behavior is visible. The threat-model review workflow uses a repository secret and a state branch, and the doc says it never executes PR code.
